  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 542 AM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe closed low responsible for yesterdays wind, thunderstorms, \nand elevated fire weather conditions is now moving eastward into \nthe Canadian Prairies. Cooler, drier, and less windy conditions \nwill be left in its wake, but there will be some lingering gusty \nnorthwesterly winds over plains east of I15. These winds will be \nmost impactful over the Milk River Valley where winds will \nroutinely gust in the 20 to 30 mph range. \n\nAlthough mostly dry conditions will prevail today, subtle \nshortwave energy and lingering instability may bring isolated \nshower and thunderstorm activity to areas along the MT/ID/WY \nborders in far southwest MT today. There may also be some passing \nlow and mid- level clouds along the Hi-Line on the backside of the\naforementioned closed low. \n\nRidging aloft begins to build in over the interior west for the \nmid- week period, but another trough approaching the Pacific NW \nwill gradually bring an increasingly southwesterly flow aloft over\nthe Northern Rockies. This will warm temperatures back above \naverage and bring a slow northward expansion of scattered shower \nand thunderstorm activity as the week progresses. \n\nThe Pacific NW trough looks to send stronger shortwaves into an \nenvironment with ML CAPE running in the 300 to 900 J/kg range and \nWednesday and Thursday. These should be the busiest convective \ndays if this set up occurs. Dry sub-cloud layers will make \nlocalized gusty winds and lightning the primary concerns with this\nactivity. \n\nThe trough eventually ejects eastward towards the Northern \nRockies sometime this weekend into early next week. While there \nare some timing and depth discrepancies among ensemble members, \nthe most likely scenario looks to be more scattered more shower \nand thunderstorm activity, cooler temperatures, and periods of \ngusty westerly winds. -RCG",
